AU  - Fang, YX
AU  - Chen, YX
AU  - Wang, DJ
AU  - Li, B
TI  - The potential of abandoned tree plantations to support the recovery of the mammal community
T2  - BIOLOGICAL CONSERVATION
AB  - Tree plantations worldwide often succumb to abandonment or little management due to shifting project goals and inconsistent funding. Whether natural regeneration in abandoned tree plantations can restore biodiversity remains imperative for conservation and restoration. To address this gap, we examined mammal community changes in a tree plantation-natural secondary forest mosaic landscape in the Qinling Mountains of China. We used camera trapping surveys spanning ten years and multispecies occupancy models to study how mammal communities change in abandoned Japanese larch plantations (Larix kaempferi). The results showed that abandoned plantations initially had lower mammal species richness when they were around 20-30 years old but recovered to a similar level as natural secondary forests after another decade of natural regeneration. Overall, herbivores showed a more pronounced recovery than carnivores and omnivores, but species responses varied. Most species were more likely to occur in older plantations, especially takins (Budorcas taxicolor), Asiatic black bears (Ursus thibetanus), and Sichuan snub-nosed monkeys (Rhinopithecus roxellana). The plantations and stand age showed a mixed effect on the co-occurrence of giant pandas (Ailuropoda melanoleuca) and other species. Our findings manifest the potential of abandoned tree plantations to support mammal communities as they age if the natural secondary forests exist in the landscape matrix. Nonetheless, the recovery of native species in tree plantations also relies on the landscape effect and the dynamics in the surrounding natural forests. Future conservation should actively manage some tree plantations if natural regeneration is hindered within the plantation, such as selective logging.
