TI  - Potential for influencing native bunchgrass restoration by manipulating soil biota
T2  - CONSERVATION AND MANAGEMENT OF NATIVE PLANTS AND FUNGI
CP  - Oregon Conference on the Conservation and Management of Native Vascular Plants, Bryophytes, and Fungi
AB  - Disturbance of semi-arid systems can result in severe disruption of soil biota and higher plant communities. In this paper we view the key functional groups of the soil food web to be bacteria, fungi, protozoa, nematodes, and cyanobacteria. We hypothesize that these are correlated to cryptobiotic crust cover and plant community composition. Composition of the soil biota and plants are likely to depend on degree of disturbance ranging from pristine (not previously grazed) to heavily disturbed (grazed). Soil biota manipulations have been initiated to determine if certain treatments will promote bunchgrass success in cheatgrass stands. Manipulations of the soil biota were achieved through treatments that differentially altered carbon availability.
