TI  - Techniques for the valorization of snipe (Gallinago gallinago, Lymnocryptes minimus) habitats through open land management
T2  - GIBIER FANE SAUVAGE - GAME AND WILDLIFE, VOL 15 (SPECIAL NUMBER PTS 2 AND 3) 1998
CP  - IUGB XXIIIrd Congress
AB  - Participants in a seminar on prairial wetlands held in Paris, March 28, 1995, a joint venture between the International Council of Game and Wildlife Conservation (CIC), Migratory Birds of the Western Palearctic (OMPO), the French National Hunting Agency (ONC) and the international Snipe Hunters Club (CICB), agreed on the utmost need to safeguard existing wetlands and to restore habitats now disappeared. But it appeared also absolutely essential, that these wetlands be subject to management measures implemented through vegetation control in order to preserve open land accessible to prairial birds. In order to achieve this, the implementation of a management tool of agricultural type has been recommended: either cattle grazing so as to reduce all vegetation types or mechanical mowing using adequate agricultural equipment. If the first is altogether the most natural, the less expensive and the most beneficial for soil biology one can note that it is decreasing over the whole European continent for reasons often related to the price of the meat. Therefore, it appears indispensable that, if required, the second solution be substituted to the first.:The final target being to safeguard the entire carrying capacity and habitat value of these prairial wetlands for the Snipe Gallinago gallinago, Lymnocryptes minimus, avoiding the closing of the environment.
