AU  - MORIN, PJ
AU  - WILBUR, HM
AU  - HARRIS, RN
TI  - SALAMANDER PREDATION AND THE STRUCTURE OF EXPERIMENTAL COMMUNITIES - RESPONSES OF NOTOPHTHALMUS AND MICROCRUSTACEA
T2  - ECOLOGY
AB  - In artificial-pond communities, a doubling of the density of the salamander N. viridescens dorsalis resulted in decreased adult growth and reduced production of metamorphs. Experimental manipulations of the abundance of a trophic resource, hatchling tadpoles, had no effect on density-dependent growth and reproductive success in Notophthalmus. Experimental differences in salamander density depressed the abundance and altered the species composition of tadpoles and microcrustaceans. Accidental introductions of small numbers of Notophthalmus eggs from a permanent pond resulted in the production of neotenic individuals in some control populations without adult newts. Intraspecific density is probably important in population regulation and life history variation in a keystone vertebrate predator with a complex life cycle.
