TI  - Prosopis Invasions in Eastern Africa's Rangelands: Impacts and Management Challenges
T2  - ECOLOGY AND MANAGEMENT OF INVASIVE PROSOPIS TREES IN EASTERN AFRICA
AB  - This chapter reviews information on the impacts of invasive alien Prosopis trees on rangelands in eastern Africa. Natural rangelands cover 3.3 million km(2) (about 47%) of eastern Africa and support a variety of activities, including livestock production and wildlife-based tourism. In the region, pastoral practices contribute between 10% and 44% and tourism between 9% and 13% of the gross domestic product of eastern African countries respectively. Alien Prosopis trees were introduced into the region in the early 20th century with the aim of improving rangeland productivity by providing an additional source of fodder and shade, and to combat erosion. However, these potential benefits have largely been overshadowed by negative impacts associated with invasion, resulting in declines in rangeland carrying capacity rather than enhancing it. Invasion has been shown to reduce palatable grasses through competition, sometimes totally. Domestic and wild mammalian grazers therefore have to consume excessive amounts of Prosopis pods, leading to direct injury and toxic effects, and unhealthy imbalances in their diet. As a result, there have been significant declines in livestock and wildlife populations in invaded areas in eastern Africa, leading in turn to substantial negative impacts on the quality of life of nomadic pastoralists and the sustainability of their livelihoods. Invasion of rangelands by Prosopis juliflora in eastern Africa is one of several threats to the continued practice of rangeland-based pastoralism in the region, and it will be important to contain further spread to prevent substantial economic losses.
